  "sections": [
    {
      "contentId": 17592186199538,
      "id": "34fe2a10-84f1-513b-9da5-dc3bb4e754cd",
      "kind": "tutorial",
      "knowledgePoint": null,
      "markdown": "Suppose that $U$ is a universal set and $A\\subseteq U$. Then, we have the following basic properties:\n\n- The intersection of a set and its complement gives the empty set:\n$A\\cap \\overline{A}= \\emptyset$\n- The union of a set and its complement gives the universal set:\n$A \\cup \\overline{A} = U$\n- The complement of the complement of a set gives the original set:\n$\\overline{\\overline{A}}=A$\n- The complement of the universal set is the empty set:\n$\\overline{U}= \\emptyset$\n- The complement of the empty set is the universal set:\n$\\overline{\\emptyset}= U$\n\nWe'll prove these properties in future lessons. For now, let's get some practice at applying them.",
      "mathAcademyId": "8115",
      "stepId": "17592186199084",
      "title": "Introduction"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186198134,
      "id": "0a7a3f00-d300-5972-a631-fdf9b7f3b0da",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186198276,
        "title": "Identifying Elementary Properties of Union, Intersection, and Complement"
      },
      "markdown": "Given that $R$ is a subset of a universal set $U$, simplify the following expression:\n$\\overline{R} \\cap R$",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-12983",
      "stepId": "17592186199085",
      "title": "Identifying Elementary Properties of Union, Intersection, and Complement",
      "workedSolution": "The intersection of a set and its complement gives the empty set.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\overline{R} \\cap R = \\emptyset\n$$"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186199589,
      "id": "77e409b1-cd57-522b-82d7-009d4870d8df",
      "kind": "tutorial",
      "knowledgePoint": null,
      "markdown": "Given that $U$ is a universal set and $A \\subseteq U$, we have the following additional properties:\n\n- The intersection of $A$ with the empty set equals the empty set:\n$A\\cap \\emptyset = \\emptyset$\n- The union of $A$ with the empty set equals $A{:}$\n$A \\cup \\emptyset = A$\n- The union of $A$ with the universal set equals the universal set.\n$A\\cup U = U$\n- The intersection of $A$ with the universal set equals $A{:}$\n$A \\cap U = A$",
      "mathAcademyId": "8349",
      "stepId": "17592186199086",
      "title": "Unions and Intersections With the Universal and Empty Sets"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186199591,
      "id": "9fa4f50d-ddc6-573e-a425-8d4193575f82",
      "kind": "tutorial",
      "knowledgePoint": null,
      "markdown": "We also have two **idempotent** properties of union and intersection.\n\n- The union of the set with itself gives the original set:\n$A \\cup A = A$\n- The intersection of the set with itself gives the original set:\n$A \\cap A = A$",
      "mathAcademyId": "8351",
      "stepId": "17592186199087",
      "title": "Idempotent Properties of Union and Intersection"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186198133,
      "id": "b711cb2c-e057-5e01-b6bf-922bc9f6a4d1",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186198277,
        "title": "Applying the Properties of Union and Intersection"
      },
      "markdown": "Simplify the expression $((A \\cup A) \\cap A) \\cup B$.",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-12982",
      "stepId": "17592186199088",
      "title": "Applying the Properties of Union and Intersection",
      "workedSolution": "First, the expression $A \\cup A$ simplifies to just $A$. So, the given expression can be simplified to\n\n$$\n(A \\cap A) \\cup B\n$$\n\nThen, the expression $A \\cap A$ simplifies to just $A$. So, the expression can be further simplified to\n\n$$\nA \\cup B\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n((A \\cup A) \\cap A) \\cup B = A \\cup B\n$$"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186199590,
      "id": "525e5350-dde1-5fac-856e-6eaa3bfb2f5c",
      "kind": "tutorial",
      "knowledgePoint": null,
      "markdown": "Let $A$, $B$, and $C$ be subsets of a universal set $U$.\n\n- The **associative** properties of union and intersection state the following:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A\\cup B)\\cup C & =A\\cup (B\\cup C) \\\\ (A\\cap B)\\cap C & =A\\cap (B\\cap C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- The **commutative** properties of union and intersection state the following:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cup B & =B\\cup A \\\\ A\\cap B & =B\\cap A\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "mathAcademyId": "8350",
      "stepId": "17592186199089",
      "title": "Associative and Commutative Properties of Union and Intersection"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186198132,
      "id": "fa72fe3f-719a-5fe6-9c4b-9386fd4b8077",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186198278,
        "title": "Applying the Associative and Commutative Properties of Union and Intersection"
      },
      "markdown": "Simplify the expression $A \\cap (\\overline{B} \\cap A)$.",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-12981",
      "stepId": "17592186199090",
      "title": "Applying the Associative and Commutative Properties of Union and Intersection",
      "workedSolution": "Using the commutative, associative, and idempotent properties of intersection, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA∩(B^(―)∩A) &= A∩(A∩B^(―)) \\\\\n&= (A∩A)∩B^(―) \\\\\n&= A∩B^(―)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    }
  ],
